 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q eviction
+
+The cache uses 2Q: two in-memory queues over the same set of resident entries, sharing the 12 GiB budget.
+
+- **A1 (first-use queue)** holds entries admitted but not yet reused, in FIFO order.
+- **Am (reuse queue)** holds entries requested at least once since admission, in LRU order.
+
+A newly admitted sheet enters the tail of A1. A cache hit on an entry in A1 promotes it to the head of Am; a cache hit on an entry in Am moves it to the head of Am. When admission would exceed the 12 GiB bound, the worker evicts from the tail of A1 first; only if A1 cannot free enough does it evict the least-recently-used entries from Am. A one-off screening therefore churns A1 and cannot displace a comparison sheet that has been reused into Am until A1 is exhausted.
+
+The split of the 12 GiB budget between A1 and Am is a tunable parameter, set per deployment. All 2Q bookkeeping is in-memory and private to the worker; a worker restart resets the queues, and the disk cache stays within its bound by re-admitting through A1.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (first-use FIFO queue plus reuse LRU queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps reused comparison sheets resident through long one-off screening scans: a scanned sheet stays in A1 and is evicted before any reused sheet, whereas LRU lets a sequential scan evict the working set and aged frequency counts can retain stale favorites across a project switch. The cost — a second queue and a tunable budget split — is private to the worker. |
+
